d process Lexer.fsl and generate Lexer.fs. Then the F# compiler should compile Lexer.fs. But the error is about Lexer.fsl being compiled as F# code.\n\nWait - maybe the issue is that FsLex is NOT generating the file to the project root, but to obj/, and then the Compile Include=\"Lexer.fs\" can't find it. But we already confirmed the generated files ARE in the project root.\n\nLet me look at this differently. The error message says the F# compiler is trying to compile Lexer.fsl. This could happen if:\n1. The FsLex task failed silently and didn't generate the file\n2. The generated file is in a different location\n3. There's a conflict\n\nActually, I think the issue might be that the FsLex task DID generate Lexer.fs, but the F# compiler is ALSO trying to compile Lexer.fsl because it's being picked up by SDK auto-import. The Microsoft.NET.Sdk might be auto-importing .fsl files.\n\nLet me check if there's a way to exclude the .fsl file from compilation. I might need to add `<None Include=\"Lexer.fsl\" />` or use a different approach.\n\nActually, looking at the FsLexYacc documentation more carefully, the issue might be that the .fsl file needs to be explicitly excluded from compilation. Let me try adding `<None Include=\"Lexer.fsl\" />` to the fsproj.\n\nWait, actually I think the real issue is simpler. The FsLex task generates the file, but the F# compiler might be picking up the .fsl file as a source file. I need to make sure the .fsl file is NOT compiled as F# source.\n\nLet me try a different approach - I'll check if the generated Lexer.fs file is correct and if the build error is actually about something else.\n\nActually, re-reading the error more carefully: the error is `FS0010: Incomplete structured construct at or before this point in definition. Expected '=' or other token.` at line 2, column 14 of Lexer.fsl.\n\nLine 2 of Lexer.fsl is `module Lexer`. Column 14 would be after \"module Lexer\". The F# compiler is trying to parse this as F# code and expecting a 