{"id": "cd8630d9-01ba-4dc2-8d66-76d093642b17", "timestamp": "2026-06-04T17:30:52.191693", "source": "agent", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"thought": "The F# compiler is being invoked and it's trying to compile Lexer.fsl. But the FSharpSourceCodeCompileOrder doesn't include it. So where is it coming from?\n\nWait - I see \"Input files: Lexer.fsl\" which is from the CallFsLex target. The CallFsLex target is being skipped because the output is up-to-date. But the error is still happening.\n\nLet me look at this more carefully. The F# compiler error is about Lexer.fsl. But the FSharpSourceCodeCompileOrder only has:\n- obj/Debug/net10.0/Calc.AssemblyInfo.fs\n- obj/Debug/net10.0/.NETCoreApp,Version=v10.0.AssemblyAttributes.fs\n- Parser.fsi\n- Parser.fs\n- Lexer.fs\n- Program.fs\n\nSo Lexer.fsl is NOT in the compile list. But the error is still about Lexer.fsl.\n\nWait - maybe the issue is that the F# compiler is reading Lexer.fsl as a reference or something? Or maybe the error is from a previous build that's cached?\n\nLet me try a clean build: `dotnet clean && dotnet build`\n\nActually, looking at the output again:\n```\nSkipping target \"CallFsLex\" because all output files are up-to-date with respect to the input files.\nInput files: Lexer.fsl\n```\n\nThe CallFsLex target is being skipped. But the generated Lexer.fs file exists. So the F# compiler should be compiling Lexer.fs, not Lexer.fsl.\n\nBut the error says `Lexer.fsl(2,14)`. This is very confusing.\n\nWait - maybe the issue is that the F# compiler is reading the .fsl file as a source file because it's being picked up by the SDK's auto-discovery. Let me check if the SDK is globbing for .fsl files.\n\nActually, I just realized something. The F# compiler might be reading the .fsl file because it's being included as a source file by the F# targets. The Microsoft.FSharp.Targets might have a glob that includes .fsl files.\n\nLet me try a 